import pf.Html
import ../../Ui/Code
import ../../Ui/Heading
import ../../Ui/Nav
import ../../Generated/Sources
import ../../Generated/Paths
import ../../Ui/Button
import ../../Ui/Feature
import ../../Ui/FeatureGrid
import ../../Ui/PageCards
import ../../Ui/PageHero

## The ecosystem's overview: what a Roc Solid package is, how an app uses one, what a plugin is,
## and the packages there are, as cards in the sidebar's groups.
meta = {
	title: "The ecosystem",
	description: "Packages for Roc Solid apps: Roc modules and components you add with one line in the app's header, and plugins that add tables, jobs and scripts to the server. Twenty-six so far, among them sign-in, Stripe payments, Claude from the server, charts, forms, intl, Markdown, video, jobs and cron, email, an admin, guards, storage, Web Push and OpenTelemetry.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "packages", label: "The packages" },
		{ id: "halves", label: "What is in a package" },
		{ id: "use", label: "Adding one to an app" },
		{ id: "plugins", label: "Plugins" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The ecosystem"
				title="Packages: Roc modules and components for any Roc Solid app"
				actions={[
					<Button href={Paths.ecosystem_charts} primary>Charts</Button>,
				]}
			>
				A package is a directory of Roc modules, often with components, added to an app with one line in
				its header. Use one for what the platforms leave out: charts, form checks, translations, a job
				queue, email, an admin for your tables. Some packages are also plugins, which add tables,
				background work or scripts to the server. Twenty-six so far, each with a page here.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="packages">The packages</Heading>
			<p>Grouped by where they work: in the page, on the server, and beside the app (tools and monitoring).</p>
			{...Nav.ecosystem_docs.groups.drop_if(|group| group.name == "").map(|group|
				<>
					<h3>{group.name}</h3>
					<PageCards pages={group.pages} />
				</>
			)}

			<Heading id="halves">What is in a package</Heading>
			<p>
				Each package has its own directory under the repository's <code>packages/</code> (beside
				<code>dbcheck</code>, which <code>rocx check --sql</code> uses and no app imports). It holds Roc
				modules, components, or both.
			</p>
			<FeatureGrid>
				<Feature title="Roc modules">
					<p>
						<code>main.roc</code> is an ordinary Roc package header listing the modules. A package that only
						computes (charts, forms, intl, Markdown, QR codes) names no platform. It can't render or fetch,
						so the server, the browser and an <code>expect</code> all call the same functions. A package
						that needs the server (to send email, run jobs, read the database) names the server platform;
						more than half do. One, <a href={Paths.ecosystem_headless}>headless</a>, names the CLI
						platform: rocx uses it, apps don't.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Components">
					<p>
						<code>components/*.rocx</code> are components, such as <code>{"<BarChart>"}</code>. They import
						the platform's <code>Html</code> and <code>Solid</code>, and an app is two programs on two
						platforms (server and browser). So <code>rocx generate</code> (which <code>rocx dev</code> and
						<code>rocx build</code> run first) copies them into the app, and each half compiles them on its
						own platform.
					</p>
				</Feature>
			</FeatureGrid>
			<p>
				A server-platform package can still offer modules to the browser (audio's <code>Audio.play!</code>
				runs in a click handler). It lists them in a second header, <code>browser.roc</code>, which names
				the browser platform only if they need it. The browser's half of the app sees that header instead
				of <code>main.roc</code>. If a page imports any other module, rocx stops and names the import.
			</p>

			<Heading id="use">Adding one to an app</Heading>
			<p>
				Name the package in the app's header (<code>main.roc</code>), beside the platform. You import it by
				the name before the colon; use the package's directory name, as every app here does.
			</p>
			<Code title="main.roc" source={"app [program] { charts: \"../packages/charts/main.roc\", pf: platform \"../server/platform/main.roc\" }"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				That line covers both halves: <code>rocx generate</code> writes the browser copy's header from it, on
				the browser platform, through each package's <code>browser.roc</code> if it has one. A
				server-platform package with no <code>browser.roc</code> (Stripe, storage, PostHog) is left out of
				that copy.
			</p>
			<p>
				Then import modules and components as in any Roc program: <code>import charts.Format</code>,
				<code>import charts.BarChart</code>. A component is copied to the app's
				<code>Generated/charts/BarChart.roc</code> and the import points there. Its
				<code>{"<style>"}</code> joins the app's stylesheet, and compiler errors point at the package's file.
				The charts page's bar chart, from <code>site/Demos/BarDemo.rocx</code>:
			</p>
			<Code source={Sources.charts_use.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Each package's page shows its header line, a first example and its limits. The
				<code>README.md</code> in the package's directory is the full reference.
			</p>

			<Heading id="plugins">Plugins</Heading>
			<p>
				Some features need more than functions: a job queue needs a table and background workers, a dev
				mailbox its own pages, a sound player a script on every page. A package provides these as a
				<strong>plugin</strong>: one value, made from options, listed in your program's <code>plugins</code>.
			</p>
			<Code
				title="main.roc, in part"
				source={"import jobs.Job\nimport email.Email\n\nprogram = App.program({\n\tinit!,\n\tplugins: [Job.plugin({ jobs: App.jobs }), Email.plugin({})],\n})"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				A plugin can add database tables (applied before the app's migrations), background loops, work at
				start, development pages (only under <code>--dev</code> or <code>--debug</code>), and files it
				serves, such as a page script or a service worker. It adds no routes: every URL your app answers is
				a file under <code>routes/</code> (D-116). A package that answers requests gives you handlers to call
				from your own route files, behind their folder's middleware.
			</p>
			<p>
				A plugin can declare what it needs: an environment variable, a database, another plugin. At start
				the server checks every plugin and stops on any problem (a missing variable, a required plugin not
				listed, two files at one place), listing them all. The
				<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>server platform's page</a> covers what a plugin can add
				and how to write one.
			</p>
			<p>The packages that are plugins, and what each adds:</p>
			<ul>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_auth}>Auth</a>: <code>{"Auth.plugin({ providers })"}</code> adds its tables: sessions, users, roles and two-factor sign-in. Its handlers answer your sign-in routes (a mailed link, a provider and back, the second factor, signing out, the dev sign-in) with your <code>admit!</code>, and its middleware passes on the signed-in user.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_stripe}>Stripe</a>: <code>{"Stripe.plugin({})"}</code> adds tables of customers, checkouts, subscriptions and events, kept in step by <code>{"Stripe.webhook_route!"}</code> on your webhook route, with your <code>on_event!</code>. Its functions send people to Checkout and the Customer Portal, say who has paid, and give your own billing page the card, the invoices and cancelling.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_ai}>AI</a>: <code>{"Ai.plugin({})"}</code> reads the API key and model at start (outside <code>--dev</code>, no key, no start) and logs the model. Its functions ask Claude for an answer (whole, streamed to a page, or read into a Roc type), with the app's functions as tools. Its agents are models with a role and their own tools, and can be each other's tools.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>Jobs and cron</a>: <code>{"Job.plugin({ jobs: App.jobs, crons })"}</code> adds the queue's table, its workers, the schedules' loop, and a sweep of old finished jobs.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_email}>Email</a>: <code>{"Email.plugin({})"}</code> adds the dev mailbox under <code>--dev</code>, and logs at start whether the SMTP server answers. Outside <code>--dev</code>, no <code>SMTP_URL</code>, no start.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_push}>Push</a>: <code>{"Push.plugin({})"}</code> adds the subscriptions' table, the sending job (it needs the jobs plugin), the service worker and the page's script; under <code>--dev</code> with no VAPID keys, a key pair for the run. Three handlers answer your routes for the key and a page's subscriptions.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_guard}>Guard</a>: <code>{"FlagStore.plugin({})"}</code> keeps feature-flag overrides in a table, so they survive a restart. Rate limits and guards need no plugin.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_audio}>Audio</a>: <code>{"AudioPlayer.plugin({})"}</code> adds the player for <code>Audio.play!</code>, a script every page loads.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_vibrate}>Vibrate</a>: <code>{"Vibrator.plugin({})"}</code> adds the script that passes <code>Vibrate.pattern!</code> to the browser's vibration motor. A phone app needs none.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_posthog}>PostHog</a>: <code>{"PostHog.plugin({})"}</code> sends errors to PostHog's error tracking; its handlers answer your routes for the page's script and its report endpoint.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_otel}>OpenTelemetry</a>: <code>{"Otel.plugin({})"}</code> reads the <code>OTEL_*</code> settings at start and adds the loop that sums the app's measurements and sends them to the collector. Its middleware times every request.</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
